WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start position:0%
 
western allies say they've extracted  more than a hundred thousand people from  afghanistan in recent weeks including  citizens diplomats the military and some  of their afghan staff almost half of the  evacuees transferred through doha in the  gulf state qatar some are still there  uncertain if they'll stay or be moved on  in search of a new home dw correspondent  rebecca ritters reports  in the blistering heat at doha's  al-udaid air base this giant c-17 rolls  to a stop  with it the end of a chaotic life-saving  evacuation effort  but the cargo on this final flight from  afghanistan just some equipment and a  few journalists  qatar's civilian evacuation over for now  for many of those that came before  though this is their temporary home  muhammad mustafa was one of those able  to make it out by way of qatar he  escaped with six of his family members  like so many others they were caught  unawares when the taliban took control  of kabul  my mother suddenly called me that you  have to get to  home faster  at the first call i would  be okay i will come but  my mother that day about  10 calls that i had from my mother  because they all were scared  though now safe he's sad when he thinks  of the loved ones he was forced to leave  behind it's really hard for everyone  that you leave your friends and even  don't say goodbye to them  we had to tell nobody so i don't  tell my best friends even  it's a familiar story among those here  as relief gives way to guilt  mariam s also made it out with her  family but worries for those left back  at home  i'm just heartbroken you know even  though i have made it out of afghanistan  but my heart is broken i whenever i  think about my people my relatives my  friends who are stuck in afghanistan and  they're like caged at their homes they  don't know what to do so  i feel very bad for everyone  more than 40 000 evacuees have passed  through doha since the airlift began  qatar has served as a key transit point  and the government here has been helping  in any way it can  since the evacuation they've been fully  supplied with there's a health cleaning  on site there are activities for the  kids there's  ongoing you know stock rooms that are  filling up to make sure that people have  access to whatever they need because a  lot of people have come in either with  you know very few items or even no items  at all  most people are waiting to be  transferred to third countries mariam  doesn't yet know where she'll end up but  dreams of the day she'll be able to  return home  i don't know if if there is still you  know hope to think that one day  everything will get fine for everybody  including those who have left  afghanistan and i promise if things get  better i'll be the first you know to fly  back to my country  for now though she and the others here  have to make the most of an uncertain  future  dw correspondent rebecca ritters who  filmed that report now joins me from  doha hey rebecca what's going to happen  to the tens of thousands of people  who've been evacuated to qatar  well michael as i mentioned in the  report qatar has served as a sort of  intermediary a sort of conduit people  are coming here they're filling out  their applications doing their pcr  testing all that kind of stuff obviously  the coronavirus has made the evacuations  even harder  from here most of them will be  transferred to third countries countries  like the us germany  and canada being key locations but lots  of other countries also taking in many  refugees  more than 50 percent of the some of the  40 000 or so people that have already  come to doha have been already  sorry resettled elsewhere and for those  that are still here more will be coming  in the following days  there will be some that will stay here  in doha but that number's still unclear  poignant stories i'm sure everywhere  there we've already heard from some of  the evacuees in your report  what else have they been telling you  it's a tragedy i i don't know how else  to describe it michael talking to these  people and hearing their stories uh is  nothing short of a tragedy really um  they really speak of the heartbreak of  leaving the country i tried to convey  that in the report because whilst they  feel really lucky to be able to be in  safety now they of course  are very passionate people they're very  patriotic people they love their country  and they they really all the people i  spoke to said that they were just  heartbroken not just to leave the  country itself but also the people that  they've had to leave behind there's also  a growing sense of guilt um if i could  put it like that in that many people who  are now safe really fear for their  friends and family back at home so it's  a devastating uh  situation and of course lots of tears  shed in those storytelling qatar clearly  playing a key role at least in some  phase of this story how important is  qatar when it comes to facilitating  dialogue with the taliban  well qatar have played a key role  facilitating dialogue with the taliban  long before the takeover a short while  ago um they have been  hosting the taliban here in qatar in  doha for many years at the request of  the us and they've been holding um  lots of talks between between the  taliban and western nations as well as  intra-afghan talks with the afghan  government they obviously didn't go that  well as we've seen in the past few weeks  um but it has been a very key role  they've also played a key role in the  evacuations there's obviously had to be  negotiations with the taliban in order  to get the people that have been able to  get out out  and of course going forward qatar say  that they are still willing to try and  evacuate more people and continue those  negotiations to try and evacuate people  after that thirty first u s deadline  that may be essential that's uh rebecca  ritters from doha many thanks rebecca  for more on the humanitarian situation  in afghanistan i'm joined now by chris  meltzer from the u.n refugee agency or  unhcr here in berlin welcome back to dw  chris we've just heard there from  evacuees that they're worried about  those left behind what are the  conditions those displaced people still  inside the country are facing  some of them are really dire conditions  so we have about 3.5 million refugees  within the country so internal displaced  persons afghans who are  in afghanistan  and who are kind of refugees idps and  many of them are very dire conditions  that's why we're trying to help them  we have a small number of people who  could  could return but many of them are now  and we are facing soon fall and winter  are  need indeed international help urgently  you mentioned dire conditions what  exactly do you mean by that just paint a  picture for me  many flared from their homes and they  have sometimes nothing only what they're  wearing or what they could carry um and  that's it many of them are with  relatives yes  but  there are thousands maybe tens of  thousands even hundreds of thousands who  are who are without really support and  they need a roof they need clean water  they need food  that's what we try to provide but we  can't reach all of them  and what's the challenge uh the un  refugee agency faces as western allies  and their evacuations the uk of course  being one of the latest  well one challenge is of course that the  the country is now less secure and this  is also uh infrastructure problems um  like um giving the the trucks the  lorries with the help to the people the  other thing is the sheer number again  3.5 million  internal displaced persons so we are not  even talking about refugees outside of  afghanistan just the people inside  afghanistan this is already an enormous  challenge for for united nations and  other  relief organizations i suppose another  challenge is the decision about whether  you leave as well i mean will your  agency also leave the country  no we will stay stay and deliver that's  uh that's what we say uh i mean they are  most needed now uh we are in afghan  afghanistan for more than 40 years and  we were the the last 20 years we were  there also in the 90s when the taliban  already ruled afghanistan and  it is our intention to stay and to help  these people because they really need  our support and this is by the way our  mandate so we were made for situations  like that  that is a chris meltzer from the un  refugee agency many many thanks chris

